  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nClouds this morning have scattered out a few hours earlier than\nforecast so temperatures have warmed quicker. This has allowed \nhigher values of surface based CAPE to develop already, with \nvalues approaching 1500 J/kg in western NY already. Storms that \nhave formed south of the lake have struggled to maintain \nthemselves so far despite 40 to 45 knots of bulk shear but that \nmay be because of some mid level stability. Continued day time \nheating over the next hour or two will allow for enough \ninstability to overcome the stable layer. Hodographs are fairly \nstrait so that would mainly support hail and damaging wind gusts\nwith any of the stronger storms. \n\nWith the dry air aloft, temperatures cool quickly towards \nsunset so storms will likely become more elevated and weaken \nafter 7 pm as the surface instability wanes. The Finger Lakes \nregion has the best chance for the severe storms later today. \nNEPA will have the front passage too late at night so while a \nfew rumbles of thunder is possible, it will mostly be light to \nmoderate rain showers overnight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nTomorrow will be one of the cooler days since May behind the \ncold front and persistent northerly flow all day with highs in \nthe low to mid 60s. High pressure building in will cause winds \nto subside quickly in the afternoon with efficient radiational \ncooling tomorrow night. Lows were lowered below NBM with some \nareas of the Catskills and Tug Hill falling into the upper 30s. \nIf dew points can mix lower tomorrow afternoon, there is a low \npotential (<10%) of patchy frost in Northern Oneida county. \n\nUpper level ridging nudges back in Wednesday into the end of \nthe week with temperatures gradually warming back to near to \nslightly above normal. With the weak warm air advection and \nbeing on the northern edge of a ridge means there could be a few\nshowers and thunderstorms to deal with late week into the \nweekend as shortwaves propagate over the top of the ridge.",
